Hi,

  v3.40.0 of Generic NQS, a freely-available version of the Network Queueing
  System, is now available, in BETA form, from :
  
  ftp://ftp.shef.ac.uk/uni/projects/nqs/latest/Generic-NQS-PRE-RELEASE.tar.gz
  
  Our site is mirrored by suniste.unc.edu, where it will be available from
  
  ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/system/Network/distrib/nqs/latest/
  Generic-NQS-PRE-RELEASE.tar.gz
  
  A brief changelog for recent versions is included below.  The main thing to
  note for Linux users is that Linux/ELF is now supported from this release
  onwards.  This release was tested with libc-5.1.4, and gcc-2.7.0.
  Although this release should work with the a.out C library, this
  configuration will not be supported from now on by us (although patches
  are always welcome). 
  
  More information (including detailed ChangeLogs, bug lists, problem
  reporting, mailing list archives, etc etc) can be found from 
  
  http://www.shef.ac.uk/uni/projects/nqs/
  
  ---
  
  The Network Queueing System (NQS) is the de facto standard batch
  processing system for UNIX, with freely available versions available from 
  CERN, and the University of Sheffield, and commercial versions available
  from vendors such as Cray and Sterling Software.  
  
  Our version, Generic NQS, is the successor to the popular Monsanto-NQS
  release.  It is widely used within UK Academia, and around the world.
  There is support for a number of operating systems :

    AIX, Fujitsu, HPUX, IRIX 4,5,6, Linux, Linux/ELF, NCR, OSF/1, Solaris,
    SunOS, ULTRIX

  Generic NQS is derived from the original COSMIC NQS, by NASA, and is fully
  interoperable (at the networking level) with other versions of NQS.
  
  Generic NQS is released under version 1 of the FSF's GPL.
  
  There are a number of mailing lists available for this product - see the
  WWW URL above for further details.
  
Regards,

Stu
--  
Stuart Herbert		  Academic Computing Services, University of Sheffield
UNIX Support		      (0114) 282 4254 (External) / Ext 4254 (Internal)
